Nx [caches task results locally](/docs/features/cache-task-results) so the same machine doesn't repeat
a task with the same inputs.
Remote caching shares those results across developer machines and CI jobs.

![Three developers and CI sharing cached task results](../../../../assets/features/distributed-caching.svg)

On a remote cache hit, Nx restores the terminal output and declared task artifacts, such as a build
or distribution directory.
The command behaves like a local cache hit without running the task again.

Every cache hit is a task your CI machines don't run, so remote caching shortens pipelines and cuts machine-minutes, which reduces cost.

## Configure remote caching

Connect your workspace to Nx Cloud:

```shell
npx nx@latest connect
```

{% call_to_action title="Connect from your browser" url="https://cloud.nx.app/get-started?utm_source=nx-docs&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=nx-cloud-signup&utm_content=remote-cache" icon="nxcloud" description="Sign in to Nx Cloud and connect your repository without the CLI" /%}

For authentication and CI setup details, see [Setting Up CI](/docs/getting-started/setup-ci).

## Why use remote caching

Remote caching can reduce work in several places:

- Repeated CI runs can reuse tasks that already ran with the same inputs.
- Developers can reuse results created in CI when their cache permissions grant access.
- CI jobs and Workers can transfer task artifacts through the shared cache.

[`nx affected`](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) runs only the tasks your change reaches.
The tasks they depend on run too, and those restore from the cache when nothing in them changed.
When you push to the same pull request again, `nx affected` selects the same tasks, and the ones your new commit doesn't reach are cache hits.

Workers use remote caching to share artifacts
between machines.
When a task depends on output produced by another agent, Nx restores that output before running the
dependent task.

## What Nx Cloud stores

Each cached task result contains:

- Terminal output written to standard output and standard error.
- Files matched by the task's `outputs` configuration.
- The hash that identifies the task computation.

The cache entry doesn't contain the input source files.
Nx hashes those inputs to calculate the task hash.
For more information, see [what Nx caches](/docs/features/cache-task-results#what-nx-caches).

A task's `inputs` decide whether it hits or misses the cache, and its `outputs` decide which files are
restored on a hit.
To see which tasks read or write files outside them, open **Analytics** > **Cache inputs and outputs**
in Nx Cloud, and open a task's **Report** tab for the files each process read and wrote.
[Improve cache inputs and outputs](/docs/kb/improve-cache-inputs-outputs) walks through fixing them
with a coding agent, and on Nx Cloud, Ultracache handles most of them for you.

## Ultracache

Ultracache improves cache hit rates and keeps file inputs correct without requiring you to maintain complex configuration.
It gives you several benefits:

- **Faster CI and lower compute costs.** Changing a file a task never reads no longer invalidates its cache.
  Fewer unnecessary cache misses mean fewer tasks to run, which can substantially reduce CI compute (**up to 3× in some cases**).
- **Correct cache invalidation.** By default, Ultracache tracks every file a task reads,
  preventing stale cache hits from missing input declarations.
- **No input configuration maintenance.** Ultracache records file inputs for new targets
  automatically and keeps them up to date as your tasks change. You don't need to maintain file
  lists by hand.
- **Increased extensibility.** Ultracache automatically configures file inputs for scripts you run
  through Nx. Any new technology you add gets the same automatic configuration, without needing
  an Nx plugin.

[Manually configured inputs](/docs/kb/configure-inputs) and [inputs inferred by plugins](/docs/features/run-tasks#inferred-tasks)
can include files a task doesn't need, causing unnecessary cache misses, or omit files it does need,
causing stale cache hits. Ultracache records the task's actual file reads instead of requiring you to
keep that list precise as the task changes.

{% aside type="note" title="Works with complex workspaces" %}
Ultracache tracks file reads across project boundaries, so you can improve cache reuse in workspaces
with large projects or circular dependencies without reorganizing them first.
{% /aside %}

Ultracache is part of every Nx Cloud plan, including Hobby, and traced tasks don't use credits.

### How Ultracache works

The first time a task runs on Workers, Nx hashes it from its declared `inputs` and records the files it reads.
Later CI runs hash the task from that recording, and the recording grows when the task reads a file it didn't read before.
It lives in Nx Cloud, so your configuration files don't change, and declared `outputs` still decide what Nx caches.

Only runs on Workers are recorded, but every CI run hashes from the recording, including on CI machines you manage yourself.
Runs outside CI hash from declared `inputs`.
Cache hits, continuous tasks, tasks with `cache: false`, and tasks run by a batch executor aren't traced.

### Turn on Ultracache

New Nx Cloud workspaces start with Ultracache on.
For an existing workspace, open the workspace's [**Settings**](https://cloud.nx.app/go/workspace/settings) page in Nx Cloud and set **Ultracache** to **On**.

Ultracache requires Nx 24.0.0 or later, which uses it in CI by default.
For Nx 23 and runs outside CI, see [Ultracache and Nx versions](/docs/kb/ultracache-nx-versions).

To skip Ultracache for a single run, set [`NX_CLOUD_USE_ULTRACACHE`](/docs/reference/environment-variables) to `false`.

#### Try Ultracache on one branch or workflow

To trial Ultracache before turning it on for the whole workspace, set `NX_CLOUD_ULTRACACHE_MODE` for a single CI pipeline.
It overrides the workspace setting for that pipeline only and doesn't need an Nx upgrade.
The value is case-insensitive and maps to the [`ultracache.mode`](/docs/reference/project-configuration#ultracache) modes:

| Value   | Effect                                                                     |
| ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `ON`    | Turns Ultracache on. Targets keep their own `mode`, and the rest use `on`. |
| `WARN`  | Applies `warn` to every task, overriding each target's `mode`.             |
| `ERROR` | Applies `error` to every task, overriding each target's `mode`.            |
| `OFF`   | Turns Ultracache off for every task.                                       |
| Unset   | Uses the workspace setting.                                                |

`NX_CLOUD_ULTRACACHE_MODE` takes precedence over [`NX_CLOUD_USE_ULTRACACHE`](/docs/reference/environment-variables): with `OFF`, `WARN`, or `ERROR`, the run doesn't use recorded inputs even if `NX_CLOUD_USE_ULTRACACHE` is `true`.
Don't combine `ON` with `NX_CLOUD_USE_ULTRACACHE=false`, because Nx 24 and later still skip Ultracache for that run.
Any other value fails the run with an error.

Set it at the job or workflow level so both `nx-cloud start-ci-run` and your Nx commands see it.
Nx Cloud Workers pick it up automatically:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ultracache-trial.yml
on:
  push:
    branches: [try-ultracache]

env:
  NX_CLOUD_ULTRACACHE_MODE: 'ON'

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
      - run: npx nx-cloud start-ci-run --distribute-on="3 linux-medium-js"
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

It doesn't change which tasks run, so tasks that `nx affected` leaves out stay out.

{% aside type="note" title="The workspace setting stays the same" %}
`NX_CLOUD_ULTRACACHE_MODE` doesn't change the **Ultracache** setting saved in Nx Cloud. Other branches and pipelines keep using it.
{% /aside %}

### Turn off Ultracache for a target

Every target uses Ultracache by default.
Set `ultracache.mode` to `off` to hash a target from its declared `inputs` instead.
This also turns off tracing for the target, so its reads and writes no longer appear under
**Analytics** > **Cache inputs and outputs**:

```json
// project.json
{
  "targets": {
    "e2e": {
      "ultracache": {
        "mode": "off"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

To keep Ultracache on but leave paths such as a tool's scratch directory out of a target's recording,
list them in `ultracache.ignoreReads` and `ultracache.ignoreWrites`.
For every mode and option, see the [`ultracache` target property](/docs/reference/project-configuration#ultracache).

### Ultracache and affected

[`nx affected`](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) uses task inputs to determine which tasks a change affects and need to run.
For tasks with an Ultracache recording, those inputs come from the files the task actually reads.
This makes `nx affected` more efficient and accurate: unused files no longer select tasks unnecessarily,
and files missing from declared inputs no longer cause affected tasks to be overlooked.

## Remote cache security

A cache hit replays files and terminal output without running the task, so the main risk is cache poisoning: a tampered entry that developers and CI then use as real task output.
Nx Cloud guards against it in several ways:

- Your source code never reaches the cache. Nx Cloud stores the task hash, the files matched by `outputs`, and the terminal output.
- Cache entries are immutable. Once Nx Cloud stores an entry, no later run can overwrite it.
- CI writes are scoped by token. Use a [read-write token](/docs/kb/access-tokens#read-write-access) only on protected branches, such as `main`. On other branches, a [read-only token](/docs/kb/access-tokens#read-only-access) reads the shared cache and writes to a cache isolated to that run, so a pull request can't change what `main` restores.
- Nx Cloud protects against [CREEP (CVE-2025-36852)](https://nx.dev/blog/creep-vulnerability-build-cache-security#the-creep-vulnerability), where an untrusted pull request writes a poisoned artifact that a trusted build later restores. With the token setup above, pull request writes stay isolated from the cache used by protected branches.
- Developers authenticate with [personal access tokens](/docs/kb/personal-access-tokens). [Workspace settings](https://cloud.nx.app/go/workspace/settings/access) decide whether each developer can write to the cache, and whether unauthenticated machines get read-write, read-only, or no access.
- [End-to-end encryption](/docs/kb/encryption) encrypts artifacts on your machines before they're uploaded, with a key Nx Cloud never sees.

To keep a poisoned entry from ever reaching your users, [skip the cache](/docs/kb/skipping-cache) when you build the artifact you deploy.
For the full list of precautions, see [cache security](/docs/kb/cache-security).
Nx publishes compliance information, including SOC 2, on the [Nx security site](https://security.nx.app).

## Remote cache availability

Nx checks the local cache before requesting a result from the remote cache.
If the remote cache is unavailable, local cache hits remain available, and Nx runs tasks that aren't
cached locally.

## Enterprise remote caching

Organizations with specific data residency or infrastructure requirements can
[contact the Nx Enterprise team](https://nx.dev/enterprise/trial) to discuss remote caching deployment
options.

## Skip remote caching

Use `--skipRemoteCache` when one command shouldn't read from or write to the remote cache.
For related local cache options, see [skip task caching](/docs/kb/skipping-cache).

